---
title: Multirate Split ODE Work-Precision Diagrams
author: Harsh Singh
---

The methods in OrdinaryDiffEqMultirate.jl integrate additively split ODEs

```math
\frac{du}{dt} = f_1(u,p,t) + f_2(u,p,t)
```

where `f1` is the fast component and `f2` is the slow component (the SciML
`SplitODEProblem` convention). One macro step of size `h` evaluates the slow
rate a handful of times, while the fast rate is advanced by `m` micro steps of
size `h/m`. This is worth doing when the right-hand side cost is asymmetric: a
cheap fast component that forces small steps for stability or resolution, and
an expensive slow component that a single rate method is forced to evaluate at
the fast cadence anyway. When that asymmetry is absent, the multirate machinery
is pure overhead. This file measures both situations on purpose, so the
diagrams can be trusted in either direction.

The ten registered methods of OrdinaryDiffEqMultirate benchmarked here, with
the provenance given in their docstrings:

  - `MREEF`: multirate Richardson extrapolation built on the frozen slow
    forward Euler splitting, following the multirate extrapolation approach of
    Engstrom, Ferm, Lotstedt and Sjogreen (2009). The `order` parameter sets
    the extrapolation order; the default 4 is used here.
  - `MRAB`: multirate Adams Bashforth. The slow rate is frozen over each macro
    interval and the fast rate is advanced with a `k` step Adams Bashforth
    formula (Sandu and Constantinescu 2007). The frozen slow rate limits the
    global order, so this method lives in the loose tolerance regime.
  - `MRIGARKERK22a`, `MRIGARKERK22b`, `MRIGARKERK33a`, `MRIGARKERK45a`:
    explicit multirate infinitesimal GARK methods of orders 2, 2, 3 and 4 from
    Sandu (2019, SIAM Journal on Numerical Analysis 57(5)).
  - `MRIGARKIRK21a`, `MRIGARKESDIRK34a`, `MRIGARKESDIRK46a`: solve-decoupled
    implicit MRI-GARK methods of orders 2, 3 and 4 from the same paper. The
    fast component is micro stepped explicitly and the slow component is
    handled implicitly, so these target problems whose slow component is itself
    stiff.
  - `MIS`: the MIS2(4,2) multirate infinitesimal step tableau of Wensch, Knoth
    and Galant (BIT 2009).

All ten methods are declared time adaptive with embedded error estimates.
Measurement says two of them cannot be benchmarked through their controllers on
these problems: MRAB's estimate does not see the frozen slow error and its
achieved accuracy stops responding to the tolerance, and MRIGARKERK22b's
estimate does not see micro instability so it diverges silently at loose
tolerances. Those two are run at fixed macro steps through the `dts` mechanism
of `WorkPrecisionSet`, and this is called out again at the figures involved.
Everything else is a plain tolerance sweep.

The linearly implicit extrapolated multirate member is not registered yet and
will be added to this file in a follow up once it is released.

```julia
using OrdinaryDiffEqMultirate, OrdinaryDiffEqTsit5, OrdinaryDiffEqVerner,
      OrdinaryDiffEqLowOrderRK, OrdinaryDiffEqRosenbrock, OrdinaryDiffEqSDIRK
using DiffEqDevTools, SciMLBase, SciMLLogging, LinearAlgebra, Plots
gr()
```

## Problem 1: cheap stiff fast part, expensive slow part

This is the regime multirate methods are built for. `N` phases sit in a stiff
relaxation toward a slowly rotating target (the fast part, diagonal, `O(N)`
cost, rate `lambda`), coupled through a dense Kuramoto style interaction (the
slow part, `O(N^2)` cost, `O(1)` magnitude and timescale):

```math
u_i' = -\lambda \left( u_i - \cos(t + \theta_i) \right)
     + \frac{K}{N} \sum_{j=1}^{N} \sin(u_j - u_i)
```

The solution moves with `O(1)` amplitude for the whole integration, so the
error being measured is meaningful at every tolerance. An explicit single rate
method is stability limited to `dt` of about `2/lambda` and pays the dense
`O(N^2)` sum on every one of those steps. A multirate method takes macro steps
sized by the slow dynamics and pays the dense sum only there. The dense sum is
evaluated naively as a controlled stand-in for a slow right hand side that is
genuinely expensive per evaluation; this particular interaction admits a
trigonometric factorization down to `O(N)` work, unlike the nonlocal kernel of
problem 3, so the cost ratio here is by construction.

```julia
const N = 96
const lam = 1000.0
const K = 1.0
const theta = [2pi * i / N for i in 1:N]

function fast1!(du, u, p, t)
    @inbounds for i in 1:N
        du[i] = -lam * (u[i] - cos(t + theta[i]))
    end
    nothing
end

function slow1!(du, u, p, t)
    @inbounds for i in 1:N
        s = 0.0
        ui = u[i]
        for j in 1:N
            s += sin(u[j] - ui)
        end
        du[i] = (K / N) * s
    end
    nothing
end

function full1!(du, u, p, t)
    fast1!(du, u, p, t)
    @inbounds for i in 1:N
        s = 0.0
        ui = u[i]
        for j in 1:N
            s += sin(u[j] - ui)
        end
        du[i] += (K / N) * s
    end
    nothing
end

u0 = [cos(theta[i]) for i in 1:N]
tspan = (0.0, 3.0)
prob1 = SplitODEProblem(fast1!, slow1!, u0, tspan)
prob1plain = ODEProblem(full1!, u0, tspan)
probs1 = [prob1, prob1plain]

du0 = similar(u0)
fast1!(du0, u0, nothing, 0.5); slow1!(du0, u0, nothing, 0.5)
tf = @elapsed for _ in 1:2000; fast1!(du0, u0, nothing, 0.5); end
ts = @elapsed for _ in 1:2000; slow1!(du0, u0, nothing, 0.5); end
println("slow/fast cost ratio: ", round(ts / tf, digits = 1))
```

The multirate methods receive the `SplitODEProblem`; the single rate reference
methods receive the plain unsplit `ODEProblem` (`full1!`) so they are not
charged for the split function machinery and are compared in the form a user
would actually run them. The reference solution is computed at `1e-13`
tolerances and cross checked against a second solver of a completely different
family:

```julia
sol_ref1 = solve(prob1plain, Vern9(), abstol = 1e-13, reltol = 1e-13)
sol_check = solve(prob1plain, Rodas5P(), abstol = 1e-12, reltol = 1e-12)
println("reference agreement: ",
    norm(sol_ref1.u[end] - sol_check.u[end]) / norm(sol_ref1.u[end]))
test_sol1 = TestSolution(sol_ref1);
```

```julia
plot(sol_ref1, idxs = 1:12:N, legend = false, size = (900, 500),
    title = "Problem 1 solution (every 12th component)")
```

### Choosing the rate ratio m

The inner step count `m` is the main tuning knob of every method here. The
micro integrators are explicit, so `m` must at least satisfy the micro
stability bound `(h_sub / m) * lambda <= C` on each fast sub interval of length
`h_sub`; beyond that, larger `m` only adds micro work. A sweep over `m` on one
MRI-GARK member and on MREEF makes the tradeoff visible:

```julia
abstols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (6:9)
reltols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (4:7)
setups = [
    Dict(:alg => MRIGARKERK33a(m = 8)),
    Dict(:alg => MRIGARKERK33a(m = 16)),
    Dict(:alg => MRIGARKERK33a(m = 64)),
    Dict(:alg => MREEF(m = 2, order = 4)),
    Dict(:alg => MREEF(m = 4, order = 4)),
    Dict(:alg => MREEF(m = 8, order = 4)),
]
names = ["MRIGARKERK33a m=8", "MRIGARKERK33a m=16", "MRIGARKERK33a m=64", "MREEF m=2", "MREEF m=4", "MREEF m=8"]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob1, abstols, reltols, setups;
    names = names, appxsol = test_sol1, save_everystep = false, dense = false,
    verbose = SciMLLogging.None(), numruns = 5, maxiters = Int(1e6))
plot(wp, title = "Problem 1: rate ratio sweep", legend = :outertopleft, size = (900, 500))
```

The two families respond to `m` in opposite ways. The `MRIGARKERK33a`
controller picks essentially the same macro steps for every `m` in this range
(the macro error is binding, not micro stability), so the achieved error is
unchanged while runtime scales with the micro work: at the tightest tolerance
`m = 8` finishes in about 0.49 seconds against 1.66 seconds for `m = 64`. For
MREEF, larger `m` improves the base solutions feeding the extrapolation, so
`m = 8` is both two orders of magnitude more accurate and 2.6 times faster
than `m = 2` at the loose end. The remaining problem 1 figures use `m = 16`
for the MRI-GARK and MIS members rather than the faster `m = 8` as a stability
margin. At `reltol = 1e-3` the controller accepts macro steps near 0.11,
past the measured micro stability edge for `m = 8` (about 0.06) and close
enough to the `m = 16` edge (about 0.12) that both settings diverge, which is
why the sweep starts at `1e-4`; there the accepted steps stay near 0.025,
well inside both edges, and `m = 16` doubles the distance to the edge at a
cost of about a third more runtime at matched tolerance. MREEF uses `m = 8`.

### Explicit multirate methods, loose tolerances

The order 1 and 2 members are compared at loose tolerances against `BS3` and
`Tsit5`. Two of the four multirate members are run at fixed macro steps rather
than by tolerance, for measured reasons. MRAB's embedded estimate only sees the
fast error, so its adaptive error stalls near the frozen slow error no matter
the tolerance; a `dts` sweep produces its actual first order work precision
line. MRIGARKERK22b's embedded estimate does not detect micro instability, and
at loose tolerances the controller accepts macro steps whose inner explicit
midpoint integration diverges (the solve returns with an error around `1e100`);
at fixed macro steps inside the micro stability bound it behaves properly.
MIS and MRIGARKERK22a have safe controllers on this problem and are run
adaptively.

```julia
abstols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (3.5:0.5:5.0)
reltols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (1.5:0.5:3.0)
setups = [
    Dict(:alg => MRAB(k = 2, m = 16), :adaptive => false,
        :dts => [0.012, 0.006, 0.003, 0.0015]),
    Dict(:alg => MIS(m = 16)),
    Dict(:alg => MRIGARKERK22a(m = 16)),
    Dict(:alg => MRIGARKERK22b(m = 16), :adaptive => false,
        :dts => [0.008, 0.004, 0.002, 0.001]),
    Dict(:alg => BS3(), :prob_choice => 2),
    Dict(:alg => Tsit5(), :prob_choice => 2),
]
names = ["MRAB (fixed dt)", "MIS", "MRIGARKERK22a", "MRIGARKERK22b (fixed dt)", "BS3", "Tsit5"]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(probs1, abstols, reltols, setups;
    names = names, appxsol = [test_sol1, test_sol1], save_everystep = false,
    dense = false, verbose = SciMLLogging.None(), numruns = 5,
    maxiters = Int(1e6))
plot(wp, title = "Problem 1: low order, loose tolerances", legend = :outertopleft, size = (900, 500))
```

Every multirate member beats both single rate references across this range,
and the margin is large. `BS3` and `Tsit5` are stability limited at about 1200
and 900 steps no matter the tolerance, so their curves sit nearly flat at
0.12 and 0.17 seconds with final errors between `3e-5` and `5e-4`; the
tolerance changes their accuracy barely at all because the step size cannot
respond to it. MRAB at its largest fixed step reaches `4.5e-6` in 0.010
seconds, which is twelve times faster than `BS3` at sixty times lower error,
and its first order line continues cleanly to `3.8e-7` at 0.082 seconds. MIS
and `MRIGARKERK22a` reach errors near `3e-10` within about two seconds; their
weakness is cost growth, roughly ten times the macro steps per tolerance
decade, which is why this figure stops at `1e-3`. `MRIGARKERK22b` at fixed
steps runs a clean second order line between MRAB and MIS.

### Explicit multirate methods, tight tolerances

The order 3 and 4 members against `Tsit5` and `Vern7`. The range starts at
`1e-4` because at `1e-3` the MRI-GARK controllers accept macro steps beyond the
micro stability bound on this problem and diverge (the same estimator blindness
noted above for `MRIGARKERK22b`):

```julia
abstols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (6:9)
reltols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (4:7)
setups = [
    Dict(:alg => MREEF(m = 8, order = 4)),
    Dict(:alg => MRIGARKERK33a(m = 16)),
    Dict(:alg => MRIGARKERK45a(m = 16)),
    Dict(:alg => Tsit5(), :prob_choice => 2),
    Dict(:alg => Vern7(), :prob_choice => 2),
]
names = ["MREEF m=8", "MRIGARKERK33a", "MRIGARKERK45a", "Tsit5", "Vern7"]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(probs1, abstols, reltols, setups;
    names = names, appxsol = [test_sol1, test_sol1], save_everystep = false,
    dense = false, verbose = SciMLLogging.None(), numruns = 5,
    maxiters = Int(1e6))
plot(wp, title = "Problem 1: high order, tight tolerances", legend = :outertopleft, size = (900, 500))
```

MREEF at `m = 8` is the method to beat in this regime. It reaches `1.6e-8` in
0.075 seconds where `Vern7` needs about 0.22 seconds for a comparable `7.6e-8`
and `Tsit5` needs 0.31 seconds to get to `7e-9` at all. `MRIGARKERK33a` wins
its band as well: `4.5e-6` in 0.029 seconds against `Tsit5` at `6.5e-6` in
0.174 seconds, a six times speedup at the loose end. The advantage narrows as
the tolerance tightens, because the multirate macro step shrinks toward the
fast scale while the single rate cost is already paid: at the tightest point
`Vern7` reaches `2.6e-10` in 0.37 seconds against MREEF's `2.0e-10` in 0.55
seconds, so the seventh order single rate method takes the very tight end.
`MRIGARKERK45a` shows order reduction here, with its error nearly flat from
`1e-4` to `1e-6` tolerances, and only its tightest point is competitive. The
summary is that multirate wins by 2x to 6x through the practical tolerance
range and hands over to high order single rate methods around final errors of
`1e-9`.

### Implicit multirate methods

The solve-decoupled implicit members are also run on problem 1. Their nonlinear
solves act on the slow component, which is not stiff here, so this measures
their overhead in a regime that does not need them. `TRBDF2` and `Rodas5P` are
the single rate implicit references.

```julia
abstols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (5.5:0.5:7.5)
reltols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (3.5:0.5:5.5)
setups = [
    Dict(:alg => MRIGARKIRK21a(m = 16)),
    Dict(:alg => MRIGARKESDIRK34a(m = 16)),
    Dict(:alg => MRIGARKESDIRK46a(m = 16)),
    Dict(:alg => TRBDF2(), :prob_choice => 2),
    Dict(:alg => Rodas5P(), :prob_choice => 2),
]
names = ["MRIGARKIRK21a", "MRIGARKESDIRK34a", "MRIGARKESDIRK46a", "TRBDF2", "Rodas5P"]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(probs1, abstols, reltols, setups;
    names = names, appxsol = [test_sol1, test_sol1], save_everystep = false,
    dense = false, verbose = SciMLLogging.None(), numruns = 5,
    maxiters = Int(1e6))
plot(wp, title = "Problem 1: implicit members", legend = :outertopleft, size = (900, 500))
```

As expected, this is not the implicit members' problem. `Rodas5P` gets to
`1e-7` in 0.10 seconds; `MRIGARKIRK21a` needs 0.28 seconds to reach `1.6e-7`
and `MRIGARKESDIRK34a` is two to five times slower than `Rodas5P`
at comparable errors, only paying off below `Rodas5P`'s error floor of about
`2e-8` on this problem (it reaches `2.6e-9`, in 1.6 seconds). `TRBDF2` is the
cheapest solver here in wall time but its achieved error responds weakly and
non monotonically to the tolerance. The clear negative result is
`MRIGARKESDIRK46a`: its achieved error does not track the tolerance at all on
this problem, scattering between about `3e-4` and `5e-7` with no trend across
two tolerance decades, so while every solve completes, the method cannot be
tolerance steered here. The same method behaves properly on problem 3 below,
which points at the combination of a time dependent stiff fast component and
this method's five stage coupling structure rather than at the implementation
of the error estimate.

## Problem 2: the null case, cheap slow part

Same equations, same stiffness, same timescales, but the dense sum is replaced
by nearest neighbor coupling, so the slow part now costs `O(N)` just like the
fast part. The cost asymmetry that justifies multirate integration is gone and
single rate methods should win or tie. A benchmark that omits this case would
overstate the methods.

```julia
function slow2!(du, u, p, t)
    @inbounds for i in 1:N
        im = i == 1 ? N : i - 1
        ip = i == N ? 1 : i + 1
        du[i] = (K / 2) * (sin(u[im] - u[i]) + sin(u[ip] - u[i]))
    end
    nothing
end

function full2!(du, u, p, t)
    fast1!(du, u, p, t)
    @inbounds for i in 1:N
        im = i == 1 ? N : i - 1
        ip = i == N ? 1 : i + 1
        du[i] += (K / 2) * (sin(u[im] - u[i]) + sin(u[ip] - u[i]))
    end
    nothing
end

prob2 = SplitODEProblem(fast1!, slow2!, u0, tspan)
prob2plain = ODEProblem(full2!, u0, tspan)
probs2 = [prob2, prob2plain]
sol_ref2 = solve(prob2plain, Vern9(), abstol = 1e-13, reltol = 1e-13)
sol_check2 = solve(prob2plain, Rodas5P(), abstol = 1e-12, reltol = 1e-12)
println("reference agreement: ",
    norm(sol_ref2.u[end] - sol_check2.u[end]) / norm(sol_ref2.u[end]))
test_sol2 = TestSolution(sol_ref2);
```

On this problem the slow error no longer binds the MRI-GARK controllers, and
their estimators do not see the resulting instability: at `m = 16` both
`MRIGARKERK33a` and `MRIGARKERK45a` diverge silently even at `1e-4` and `1e-5`
tolerances, and `MRIGARKERK33a` still diverges at `1e-4` with `m = 64` since
the macro steps the controller accepts there are unstable for the stage
coupling itself. The figure therefore starts at `1e-5` and uses `m = 64`,
which is itself part of the null result: with a cheap slow part, the `m`
needed to keep the inner integration stable simply multiplies the work. MREEF
needs no such adjustment since its extrapolation based error estimate detects
the instability of its base solutions and rejects those steps on its own.

```julia
abstols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (7:10)
reltols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (5:8)
setups = [
    Dict(:alg => MREEF(m = 8, order = 4)),
    Dict(:alg => MRIGARKERK33a(m = 64)),
    Dict(:alg => MRIGARKERK45a(m = 64)),
    Dict(:alg => BS3(), :prob_choice => 2),
    Dict(:alg => Tsit5(), :prob_choice => 2),
    Dict(:alg => Vern7(), :prob_choice => 2),
]
names = ["MREEF m=8", "MRIGARKERK33a m=64", "MRIGARKERK45a m=64", "BS3", "Tsit5", "Vern7"]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(probs2, abstols, reltols, setups;
    names = names, appxsol = [test_sol2, test_sol2], save_everystep = false,
    dense = false, verbose = SciMLLogging.None(), numruns = 5,
    maxiters = Int(1e6))
plot(wp, title = "Problem 2: cheap slow part (null case)", legend = :outertopleft, size = (900, 500))
```

Single rate wins, as it should. `Vern7` reaches `1.2e-8` in 6.6 milliseconds
and `6.3e-11` in 15 milliseconds; `Tsit5` and `BS3` are similarly cheap. The
best multirate showing is MREEF, which stays within about two times of `Vern7`
at matching error (`2.1e-9` in 14 milliseconds against `Vern7` around 8
milliseconds for the same accuracy); every step of its micro and extrapolation
machinery is now overhead rather than saved slow evaluations. The MRI-GARK
members fare worse: `MRIGARKERK33a` at `m = 64` needs 0.35 seconds at the
tight end, about 25 times `Tsit5`, with the inflated `m` required just to keep
it stable, and `MRIGARKERK45a`'s tightest point is not even monotone (its
error rises from `5.6e-9` at `reltol = 1e-7` to `2.3e-8` at `1e-8`, the same
estimator blindness in milder form). Comparing this figure against the problem
1 figures is the entire
point of the pair: identical fast dynamics and identical stiffness, and the
multirate advantage appears and disappears with the cost of the slow term
alone.

## Problem 3: stiff expensive slow part, the implicit regime

The solve-decoupled implicit MRI-GARK methods treat the slow component
implicitly, so their target is a slow component that is stiff as well as
expensive, with fast dynamics that are cheap and nonstiff. Here the slow part
is a nonlinear nonlocal coupling through a Gaussian kernel, `O(N^2)`
transcendental work per evaluation with a linearized spectrum reaching about
`-2000`, and the fast part is a cheap oscillatory forcing at frequency
`omega = 40` that sets the resolution requirement for the micro integrator:

```math
u_i' = \cos(\omega t + \theta_i) + \cos(\omega t)
     + c \sum_{j=1}^{N} G_{ij} \tanh(u_j - u_i)
```

```julia
const N3 = 128
const om = 40.0
const cstiff = 2000.0
const th3 = [2pi * i / N3 for i in 1:N3]

function build_G(n)
    G = [exp(-min(abs(i - j), n - abs(i - j))^2 / 18.0) for i in 1:n, j in 1:n]
    for i in 1:n
        G[i, :] ./= sum(G[i, :])
    end
    return G
end
const G3 = build_G(N3)

function fast3!(du, u, p, t)
    @inbounds for i in 1:N3
        du[i] = cos(om * t + th3[i]) + cos(om * t)
    end
    nothing
end

function slow3!(du, u, p, t)
    @inbounds for i in 1:N3
        s = 0.0
        ui = u[i]
        for j in 1:N3
            s += G3[i, j] * tanh(u[j] - ui)
        end
        du[i] = cstiff * s
    end
    nothing
end

function full3!(du, u, p, t)
    slow3!(du, u, p, t)
    @inbounds for i in 1:N3
        du[i] += cos(om * t + th3[i]) + cos(om * t)
    end
    nothing
end

u03 = [0.5 + 0.3 * sin(th3[i]) for i in 1:N3]
tspan3 = (0.0, 1.0)
prob3 = SplitODEProblem(fast3!, slow3!, u03, tspan3)
prob3imex = SplitODEProblem(slow3!, fast3!, u03, tspan3)
prob3plain = ODEProblem(full3!, u03, tspan3)

sol_ref3 = solve(prob3plain, Rodas5P(), abstol = 1e-12, reltol = 1e-12)
sol_check3 = solve(prob3plain, Vern9(), abstol = 1e-12, reltol = 1e-12)
println("reference agreement: ",
    norm(sol_ref3.u[end] - sol_check3.u[end]) / norm(sol_ref3.u[end]))
test_sol3 = TestSolution(sol_ref3);
```

`KenCarp4` is run on a swapped split (`prob3imex`) because the IMEX convention
treats the first function implicitly, which must be the stiff coupling here.
The multirate methods use the multirate convention, fast first, and the plain
implicit references (`TRBDF2`, `Rodas5P`) get the unsplit problem.

```julia
abstols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (4:7)
reltols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (2:5)
setups = [
    Dict(:alg => MRIGARKIRK21a(m = 8)),
    Dict(:alg => MRIGARKESDIRK34a(m = 8)),
    Dict(:alg => MRIGARKESDIRK46a(m = 8)),
    Dict(:alg => TRBDF2(), :prob_choice => 2),
    Dict(:alg => Rodas5P(), :prob_choice => 2),
    Dict(:alg => KenCarp4(), :prob_choice => 3),
]
names = ["MRIGARKIRK21a", "MRIGARKESDIRK34a", "MRIGARKESDIRK46a", "TRBDF2", "Rodas5P", "KenCarp4 (imex split)"]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet([prob3, prob3plain, prob3imex], abstols, reltols, setups;
    names = names, appxsol = [test_sol3, test_sol3, test_sol3],
    save_everystep = false, dense = false, verbose = SciMLLogging.None(),
    numruns = 5, maxiters = Int(1e6))
plot(wp, title = "Problem 3: stiff slow component", legend = :outertopleft, size = (900, 500))
```

This is the regime the solve-decoupled implicit members were built for, and
`MRIGARKESDIRK46a` delivers: `2.3e-7` in 0.35 seconds, where `Rodas5P` needs
0.71 seconds for `1.2e-7`, roughly a two times win at matching accuracy, and
here its error tracks the tolerance cleanly. `MRIGARKIRK21a` runs a clean
second order line and `MRIGARKESDIRK34a` reaches the lowest error of the
figure (`4.3e-9`) though at 2.4 seconds, its controller again spending about
ten times more steps per tolerance decade than its order should need. The
single rate IMEX and SDIRK references have a bad time with the fast forcing:
`TRBDF2` never gets below `1e-3` final error in this range and `KenCarp4` on
the swapped split stalls near `1e-4`, both completing quickly but delivering
orders of magnitude less accuracy at every tolerance. The one single rate
method that holds its ground is `Rodas5P`, and the honest reading is that the
implicit multirate advantage is real but modest, about a factor of two, and
it comes bundled with the more reliable error control of the 46a member being
problem dependent.

## Conclusion

The measurements support the standard multirate claim, with sharp boundaries.
When the slow right-hand side is expensive and the stiffness lives in a cheap
fast component (problem 1, with a 70 times cost ratio), the explicit multirate
methods beat stability limited explicit single rate methods by 6x to 12x at
loose tolerances and 2x to 6x at practical tolerances, with MREEF the
strongest member overall and MRAB the cheapest at loose accuracy. High order
single rate methods reclaim the very tight end (final errors below about
`1e-9`), and a Rosenbrock method on the full problem remains the better
implicit choice when the slow part is not stiff. When the cost asymmetry is
removed (problem 2), the same multirate methods tie at best and lose by up to
25 times, so the split structure alone is not a reason to use them. When the
slow part is stiff as well as expensive (problem 3), the solve-decoupled
implicit members earn their place, with `MRIGARKESDIRK46a` about twice as fast
as `Rodas5P` at matching error while `TRBDF2` and `KenCarp4` fail to deliver
accuracy under the fast forcing.

The main practical caveat is controller maturity rather than method cost.
Several embedded estimates are blind to errors the methods actually commit:
MRAB to the frozen slow error, the explicit MRI-GARK members to inner
instability (which produced silent divergence at loose tolerances throughout
this file), and `MRIGARKESDIRK46a` to whatever drives its stall on problem 1.
Users of these methods should treat the tolerance as a knob to verify, not
trust, and the fixed step data here shows the underlying methods are sound
when the step is chosen inside their stability region.

```julia, echo = false
using SciMLBenchmarks
SciMLBenchmarks.bench_footer(WEAVE_ARGS[:folder],WEAVE_ARGS[:file])
```
